  "uid": "427285.ArVJ+uhC966iMNz3B1jyyoTSL2vLLy6s6p33kKs0sco=",
  "from_addr": "Mike Allen <mike@axios.com>",
  "subject": "☃️ Axios Finish Line: Card tricks",
  "date": "Mon, 23 Nov 2026 21:29:32 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🍷 Plus: Date night surprise | Monday, November 23, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY AMAZON \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 23, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n 🦌 Season's greetings! If you're in the middle of planning your family holiday card, this one's for you. Axios visual journalist Jim Sergent is at the helm today, sharing what he's learned from the hundreds of cards he's received over the years.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 479 words … 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Make your holiday card shine \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Michael Danzansky, Bodhi Danzansky and their dog Bear in their 2025 holiday card, which sits atop a pile of all of Jim's cards. Photo: Jim Sergent/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Call me sentimental or a holiday hoarder: I've received and saved more than 800 holiday cards over the last 25 years — and I have thoughts on them, Jim Sergent writes. \n\n Why it matters: Creating and sending holiday cards costs time and money, so it's worth making your mailer memorable. 📸 The key takeaway: I want a photo.\n\n An expensive, embossed star quickly loses its luster. But even the lowest-budget picture card — with everyone's names, please — makes me pause and think about your family every time I walk by the year's new pile of cards. Zoom in: One of my favorite cards from last year has a photo of a father, son and dog in matching holiday sweaters and dour expressions (see above) .\n\n Another has two photos: The front is a longtime friend and her son, who now towers over her. On the back is the father, husband and friend we've lost. The front and interior of Linda Epstein and Christopher Root's creative 2019 holiday card; Image: Jim Sergent/Axios A few more lessons: \n\n 💡 Give me a moment or two from the year. Please don't include a dozen tiny images of your 2026.\n\n Last year, one card ended up highlighting a Christmas tree illustration instead of the postage stamp-size images that encircled it. 🥳 Make it fun. We look forward to certain friends' and relatives' latest creative takes.\n\n One family sets up amusing moments like a faux scene where no one is focused on the bat mitzvah girl. And if you're going to enclose a letter, keep it light and short. One relative's humble brag about their new home became a dinnertime punchline for days. 🙅 Yes, but: Glitter doesn't count as fun.\n\n You don't want to be remembered by that sparkle that my son couldn't wash off for days. 🕰️ Bonus points for a time stamp, like a \"Wishing you a happy 2027!\"\n\n You never know who will want to keep you in their memories for years to come. Share this story . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AMAZON\r\n\n \r\n Meet Mark: Amazon helped him scale his sleep tech company \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n About the business: A former NASA engineer, Mark used his skills to create a sleep tech company. He started selling on Amazon to reach new customers and grow his small business.\n\n\r\n Why Amazon? “Amazon is a great place for new companies to launch a product and reach new customers.”\n\n\r\n Learn more. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🥂 Kindness du jour \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A random act of kindness on date night, shared by reader John Conway:\n\n \"After finishing a nice meal and bottle of wine at an Italian restaurant in Chattanooga, Tenn., the server came to clear the table and I asked for the check. She said, 'It's been taken care of.'\" \"Acknowledging our questioning looks, she slightly nodded to her left. We realized that the couple behind us had heard us telling the server we were celebrating our 50th wedding anniversary — and they'd picked up the entire tab.\" We were flabbergasted that complete strangers would be so forthcoming. It's the best example of 'Southern hospitality' that we've ever experienced.\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
